And pre-render and the renderer really did get faster.

**kept (coverage, close-out group B), not timed.** `c608c925`. `packInstancedRowsWasm` declined screen-space queues, a pixel-snapped atlas and a type filter, and `packInstancedRows`' slow path packed them in JS. `pack_sprites_general` and `pack_casters_general` (gpuPack.wasm) do that loop:
- the type filter before the capacity check;
- the depth and the particle count by the rows written;
- the camera's transform and turn on position, scale and rotation;
- pixel snap in world or screen space, Math.round as V8 rounds;
- each value stored as a Float32Array stores it.

Their sixteen parameters (flags, camera, snap, types) sit 16 bytes into the pack's results region, pre-render's and Pixi's, which grew from 16 to 144 bytes. The six fast exports compile byte for byte as before, so no fast path's speed moved. `gpuPackWasm.test.js`: eight slow-path cases (screen, turned, snapped in world and in screen, include and exclude filters, a mix), for sprites and casters, dense and listed, under capacity: every row bit for bit as `packInstancedRows`. What stays JS in the pack (item 6b): the Pixi batches that call `packInstancedRows` directly in Pixi-pack mode (glow, shadow, caster, custom layers) and the pose-interpolation batches, whose rows are not in the render-queue memory.

**WASM close-out step 3, group B's first batch: the emit writes every row type and its JS left `src/`; E-138's presort, the light cookies and Pixi's glow and custom-layer batches through the kernels. Kept as hygiene; pre-render on adobeAnimateScene at the minimum zoom is +2.5 % over 8 pairs, under the 3 % line.** 2026-10-25.

The owner's direction for group B: only well-tested, optimized WASM; the JS copies of the kernels leave the engine. The inventory of that day (every kernel's fallbacks with the defaults on) found the emit's JS running on these cases, now covered by the kernel:

- glow rows (type 3): LightEmitter among the emit's blocks, the light entity's Transform x and y from the collect's (019deec1);
- particle rows (type 1): the particle memory a region of the render-queue memory (a120ab97);
- LiquidFun rows (type 7): its render buffer and pre-render's previous positions there (0f05ae2f), LiquidFun's heap views on each worker's own handle of the block, since a posted SharedArrayBuffer is another object for the same memory (6e726b47; liquidFunStressScene's JS loop ran on 239 of 240 frames before);
- a persist frame's rows of other types, in a loop of their own (e50a4b96);
- Adobe assets the JS took (plain arrays copied in; no room in preRender.adobeTablesMB, malformed, or past 256 ids is an error, not drawn: 91aeb79b);
- custom sprite layers: their queues and collectors in the memory, the collector stashing the collect's poses (495507c9; platformerGameScene's bloom layer 300 of 300 frames in the kernel);
- BulletComponent without the bullet tick's regions (a15dafe7), the collect's blocks whatever wasm.preRenderCollect says (a1d48827), wasm.box2dBlock gone, a block that cannot fit an error (89b0437d).

Then the JS emit, the persist path's JS and the Adobe expansion and clocks moved to tests/reference/preRenderEmitJs.mjs (6ef34205; installEmitJs puts them on a worker for the parity tests), wasm.preRenderEmit and wasm.preRenderEmitAdobe went, and a queue the kernel cannot take is drawn empty with one error. In the same batch: E-138's presort sorts in pre-render's painter kernel (e757a165), the light cookies are a render queue in the stamp's regions packed by gpuPack.wasm (7769e579), Pixi's glow batch (pixi pack mode) and the custom layers' batches pack through gpuPack.wasm (95361ced), and Pixi packs nothing of its own on frames pre-render packed (587c5f60). The node suite passed at each commit, and every scene boots (61, source, Chrome).

**Gates** (`runPredatorAbab --vs 17028648 --hyp-rev 587c5f60 --owner preRender`, worktrees, headed, detailed stats off, AB BA order):

- Predator, 4 pairs: NEUTRAL. Pre-render 3.789 → 3.767 ms (−0.1 %, worse 2/4, cv 5/4 %); logic +0.3 %, spatial +1.5 %, renderer +1.0 %; physics +5.4 % (2/4, cv 11 %), not blocking. Load keys flat (bodies, casters, visible entities within 0.3 %).
- adobeAnimateScene at the minimum zoom, where pre-render is the bottleneck (11 ms against Pixi's 6.9): the first 4 pairs read pre-render 10.999 → 11.461 ms, +4.6 % (worse 4/4, cv 1/3 %; pairs +8.5, +1.3, +6.4, +2.8 %), and spatial +3.5 % (4/4), which the runner called REGRESSION.
- The session's A/A on that scene (587c5f60 against itself, 4 pairs): pre-render +1.4 % (pairs +0.5, −0.5, +2.4, +4.3 %), spatial +1.8 % (−2.8 to +5.2 %), physics +0.1 %, renderer −1.6 %. Detection limits (2.5 × the pairs' spread / √pairs): pre-render about 2.7–3.1 % at 4 pairs and 1.9–2.2 % at 8; spatial about 4.7 % and 3.4 %.
- 4 more pairs, a fresh sitting of the same two revisions and protocol (`--append` refuses a working tree that changed, and only tests outside `src/` had): pre-render 11.059 → 11.155 ms, +1.1 % (3/4); spatial +3.9 % (3/4); physics −3.2 %, renderer −1.1 %.
- All 8 pairs: pre-render median **+2.5 %** (mean +2.8 %), worse 7/8; against the other workers +1.9 % (6/8). By run order: B second (AB) median +5.3 %, B first (BA) +1.7 %, a run-order effect of about 2 % on this scene that the alternating order cancels in the mean.
- A profile pair with detailed stats on (2 pairs, AB) read B faster: pre-render 11.67 → 10.98 and 11.69 → 11.56 ms; pack 3.74/3.66 → 3.37/3.40 ms (−8 %), emit 4.96/4.74 → 4.73/5.12 ms, collect 1.35/1.51 → 1.43/1.51 ms. No phase shows the cost the gate reads.

Verdict: **kept (hygiene)**. Pre-render, the bottleneck, is +2.5 % over 8 pairs: under the 3 % line, near the 8-pair limit, and not in any phase timer. Open: spatial is +3.65 % over the 8 pairs (worse 7/8), just over its 8-pair limit, though no change in the batch reaches spatial's work. The batch moved regions within Box2D's block (LightEmitter, the particle memory, LiquidFun's buffers, BulletComponent, the presort keys), so a layout effect is the first suspect. Spatial is not that scene's bottleneck (5.4 ms against pre-render's 11).

**WASM L4, boot to steady step times: all JS (5477a582, the last commit before E-76) against all WASM (2d264bcf). The first 2 s are not worse with WASM; every bottleneck worker is faster from the first frames. Compiling once and posting the module (plan step 4) is not needed for speed.** 2026-10-26.

**Protocol.** `runPredatorAbab --vs 5477a582 --hyp-rev 2d264bcf`, 4 pairs per window: worktrees, headed, detailed stats off, AB BA order. The base has no frame totals, so both sides read the 100 ms samples. Two windows:
- the first 2 s from the start (`--warmup-ms 1 --duration-ms 2000`): compile, the first calls at Liftoff, the spawn;
- a steady one (`--warmup-ms 8000 --duration-ms 2000`).

**Predator, first 2 s.** Every worker is faster or equal:
- pre-render 14.36 → 7.08 ms (−52.5 %, worse 0/4);
- physics 18.69 → 10.62 ms (−43.3 %);
- particle −31.5 %, spatial −13.0 %, logic −11.9 %, renderer +0.2 %;
- presented 47.0 → 45.9 fps (−2.4 %, fewer 4/4), under the 3 % line: possibly the modules' compile in those 2 s;
- the runner says FAIL (load): the boot's spawn moves the load keys (bodies within 0.1 %).

**Predator, steady.** pre-render 6.68 → 3.85 ms (−42.3 %, 0/4), spatial −13.4 %, renderer −12.6 %, physics −3.4 %, logic −0.6 %; presented 60.0 fps on both sides.

**adobeAnimateScene at the minimum zoom, first 2 s.**
- pre-render 39.38 → 11.86 ms (−69.9 %), spatial −23.5 %;
- presented 29.6 → 34.0 fps (+16.0 %, fewer 0/4);
- renderer +93.8 %: it draws the frames pre-render now gives it;
- physics +17.0 % (worse 4/4).

**adobeAnimateScene, steady.** pre-render 42.16 → 12.22 ms (−70.3 %), presented 59.9 → 60.0 fps, renderer +161 % (load, as above), physics +7.0 % (4/4), spatial +5.9 % (2/4).

**Verdict: L4 answered.** The WASM build has no warm-up penalty in step times: its first 2 s beat the JS build's on every bottleneck worker. The grouping's "compile once, post the module" stays a structural step, not a speed fix.

**Open: physics on adobeAnimateScene,** +7 % steady and +17 % in the first 2 s. Physics is not that scene's bottleneck (7.5 ms against pre-render's 12). The WASM side runs pre-render at 60 fps instead of about 24, so the machine's other work grew; the engine block also lives in Box2D's memory. Both are suspects (L5, memory bandwidth). On Predator, physics is faster (−3.4 % steady, −43 % at boot).

`neighborAccessMicrobench` (five classes, 2 000 entities, 12 neighbours each, each class's own tick loop, three runs):
- the shipped accessors, `this.neighborCount` and `this.getNeighbor(k)`: 3.8 to 4.0 ns a neighbour read;
- the entity's row of the neighbour memory read in a local loop (`Grid._neighborData` from `_neighborOffset`, the engine's private fields): 2.35 to 2.46 ns, −39 %;
- a public `this.neighborIds` returning the entity's cached view of that row (`_neighborView()`), tried and not committed: 5.3 ns, slower than the accessors (a view per entity behind a field that starts as null).

The accessors are already two typed-array reads; what costs is the call on receivers of many classes, which the engine cannot take out of them. The one faster form needs the private fields, so there is no engine change that pays: screened. Game code that walks neighbours in a hot loop gains about 1.5 ns a read by hoisting `this.neighborCount` out of its loop condition.

**E-163 answered: no scene's grid passes 65 536 cells.** 2026-10-29, from the scenes' configs (world size over
`spatial.cellSize`, 128 by default):
- the largest is planetScene, 8 192 px at 32 px cells: 256 × 256 = 65 536 cells, which the 16-bit key still holds
  (cells 0 to 65 535), so `order_by_cell` sorts it;
- badPiggiesScene's 120 000 × 40 000 world uses 512 px cells (235 × 79 = 18 565); carScene 391 × 78 = 30 498;
  blastforge 160 × 120 = 19 200; adobeAnimate 161 × 118 = 18 998; bigWorldCullStress has no spatial worker;
- every other scene is under 20 000.

So nothing measures the lever: a game whose grid passes the limit (a 20 000 px square world at 64 px cells has 97 656)
keeps its list order and loses the gather's cache order. The coarser key (cells grouped so the index fits 16 bits)
stays the fix for that case; it needs a scene knob that builds such a world before a sitting can decide it.

**N-121 fixed (`84ad58d6`): the ABAB runner judges a GPU-bound scene by the frames Pixi draws, and the page's rate where Pixi keeps it.** 2026-11-11.

**The bug.** The runner's presented guard read MAIN_FPS, the main thread's rate. In meshUploadStressScene, a GPU-bound scene:
- the branch drew 22 % more frames (241 to 247 → 300 in 10 s);
- the page's own frames, waiting on the GPU, fell 12 % (53.6 → 47.2 fps, 4 of 4 pairs);
- the runner printed REGRESSION.

**The fix.**
- `predatorMetrics` keeps `DRAWN_FPS`: Pixi's frames over the window.
- Where Pixi draws under 0.9 of the page's rate on either side (the median over pairs), the guard judges the frames drawn, printed as `drawn`.
- Elsewhere it judges MAIN_FPS as before. E-82's case stays caught: there Pixi kept up and the page fell.
- The other count prints on its own line, reported, not judged.

**Tests** (`decideAbab.test.js`):
- meshUploadStressScene's numbers are no longer a regression;
- fewer frames drawn in such a scene are one;
- E-82's numbers, with Pixi at the page's rate, are still one;
- `DRAWN_FPS` is the frames over the window, and is absent without a window.

The old runner fails all three new tests; with the fix the file passes, 22 of 22.

